---
// /docs/install — both paths to a working install: the plain plugin install, and the
// channels-enabled launch with the EXACT dev-channels flag (AC7). The flag is load-bearing
// and copied verbatim from the repo (channels page / README / channel-launch-flag memory):
// MCP instructions load WITHOUT it, so the flag is what proves the channel is actually live.
//
// Base-path rule (#1 404 trap): internal hrefs go through withBase(); commands are literal
// strings (not links) and need no prefixing.
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/docs-layout/DocsLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../../lib/base.ts";
---

<DocsLayout
  current="install"
  title="Install"
  description="Install Agentry as a Claude Code plugin in one line — then optionally launch with the development-channels flag to bring live human↔agent channels online. Requires Node ≥ 24."
  eyebrow="Getting Started · Install"
  lede="Two paths: the plain install gets you the full conducting and memory layer; the channels-enabled launch additionally brings live human↔agent push online. The first is all you need to start."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="prose__h">The plain install</h2>
    <p>
      Agentry installs as a Claude Code plugin from its GitHub repo. From a Claude Code session:
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin marketplace add Codestz/agentry
<span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>/plugin install agentry@agentry-dev</code></pre>
    <p>
      That's the whole setup. The conducting front door (<code>/agentry:go</code>), the SDLC
      specialists, and the durable memory layer all run from here — entirely from the terminal,
      no account and no build step. Head to <a href={withBase("docs/quickstart")}>Quickstart</a>
      to hand it a first task.
    </p>

    <div class="note">
      <span class="note__key">Requires</span>
      <span>Node ≥ 24 — the memory engine uses Node's built-in <code>node:sqlite</code>, so there are no native modules to compile.</span>
    </div>

    <h2 class="prose__h">The channels-enabled launch</h2>
    <p>
      Channels add <strong>live human↔agent push</strong> — comments, threaded replies, a
      permission relay, and status steering — on top of the install above. They are an additive
      layer and a research preview: to bring them online, launch Claude Code with the
      development-channels flag:
    </p>
    <pre class="flag"><code><span class="flag__sigil" aria-hidden="true">$ </span>claude --dangerously-load-development-channels plugin:agentry@agentry-dev</code></pre>

    <h3 class="prose__h prose__h--sub">Why the flag — and what happens without it</h3>
    <p>
      The flag is load-bearing in a subtle way: the channel's <strong>MCP instructions load
      without it</strong>, so seeing them is <em>not</em> proof the channel is live. The flag is
      what actually brings the channel up — it is the thing that proves the channel is running, not
      just described.
    </p>
    <p>
      Leaving the flag off is a <strong>graceful degradation</strong>, not a breakage. Everything
      still works via the async file-watch loop: the files under <code>.agentry/work/</code> are
      the source of truth, the agent reads your edits on its next turn, and tool-use approvals fall
      back to the terminal. You only need the flag when you want the <em>live</em> push instead of
      the next-turn loop.
    </p>

    <div class="note note--warn">
      <span class="note__key">Preview</span>
      <span>The <code>--dangerously-load-development-channels</code> flag is a research preview — copy it verbatim, and expect it to change before 1.0.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="prose__cta">
      <p>
        Next: <a href={withBase("docs/channels")}>Channels</a> for what live push gives you, and
        <a href={withBase("docs/permissions")}>Permissions</a> for how the relay approves a tool
        call from the Workbench.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
